<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William MacGregor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1846">1846</a>–<a href="/years/1919">1919</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William MacGregor was a Scottish physician and colonial governor. As administrator of British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-guinea">New Guinea</a> from 1888 to 1898 he explored the interior, set up government stations, appointed village constables and restricted land sales to Europeans, foundations later taken over by Australian Papua; he went on to govern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a> and Queensland.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-macgregor.jpg" alt="William MacGregor" width="320" height="415" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_William_MacGregor.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>MacGregor was born on 20 October 1846 at Hillockhead, in the parish of Towie in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberdeenshire">Aberdeenshire</a>, the eldest son of John MacGregor, a crofter, and his wife Agnes. He taught at Tillyduke and worked as a farm labourer before his schoolmaster and the local doctor encouraged him to study. He entered Aberdeen Grammar School in 1866 and the University of Aberdeen in 1867, graduating in medicine in 1872 and taking his MD in 1874, and became a medical assistant at the Royal Lunatic Asylum in Aberdeen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Contemporaries described him as sincere, discriminating and courageous, and he spoke Italian, French and German. As Governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a> from 1904 to 1909 he faced a general election that returned two parties in equal number. He refused the premier, Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-bond">Robert Bond</a>, a dissolution before the House had sat; after Bond's defeat in the House he suggested Bond resign, and a second election returned Sir Edward Morris with a good majority. Many authorities approved MacGregor's conduct.</p><p>He was appointed Governor of Queensland on 2 December 1909. On 28 June 1913, himself a pioneer of tropical medicine, he opened the new building of the Institute of Tropical Medicine in Townsville, and in August 1913 he chaired the first meeting of the Historical Society of Queensland and became its patron. He retired in July 1914 and was made a privy councillor that year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1883 he had married Mary Jane Cocks, daughter of the harbourmaster of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levuka">Levuka</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a>. In retirement he lived at Chapel-on-Leader in Berwickshire. The deaths in 1918 of his son-in-law, Admiral Sir Alfred Paget, and of his daughter Alpina made his last year a sorrowful one. He died after an operation on 3 July 1919 and was buried beside his parents at Towie.</p><p>He left his ethnological and ornithological collections to the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberdeen">Aberdeen</a>. Specimens gathered in his Papuan years went to the Queensland Museum, a museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sydney-nova-scotia">Sydney</a> and the botanical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melbourne">Melbourne</a>. The suburbs of MacGregor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brisbane">Brisbane</a> and Macgregor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canberra">Canberra</a> are named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “William MacGregor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Administrator <b>William MacGregor</b>, from 1888 to 1898, explored the interior, set up government stations, appointed village constables and restricted land sales to Europeans.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-new-guinea">British New Guinea</a></li><li><blockquote>It was the first overseas territory governed by Australia, and it took over the government stations and village constable system built up under <b>William MacGregor</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/territory-of-papua">Territory of Papua</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Berwickshire, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=57,-5&amp;year=1893" aria-label="Scotland on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4442 230 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of William MacGregor"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4892 500h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4892 500h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=57,-5&amp;year=1893">What was here in 1893? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
